  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n  We use a hybrid optimization approach combining stochastic global search with deterministic local refinement to escape local minima and converge to lower C5 values. The key insight is to balance exploration and exploitation by using global exploration with random mutations and local refinement using gradient information. This differs from previous methods by adaptively adjusting the search strategy based on the optimization landscape, using temperature-based acceptance criteria and gradient descent polishing.\n\n  Mathematical specification\n  Let h be an n-point step function on [0,2] with \u222bh(x)dx=1. We represent h as a vector of length n. The objective is to minimize C5 = max_k ||h(x)(1-h(x+k))|| dx.\n  We define the objective function F(h) = max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')*dx)\n  Constraints: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i, and sum(h) = n/2.\n  We use finite difference gradient estimation and simulated annealing with adaptive neighborhood size.\n\n  Implementation procedure\n  1. Initialization\n      - Read initial_h_values or use random initialization if not available\n      - Compute initial C5 value\n      - Set parameters: n, dx=2/n, decay_coefficient=0.96, alpha=0.5\n\n  2. Global Adaptation Phase\n      - Initialize: current_h = initial_h, current_value = F(h)\n      - For each iteration up to 1000 or time budget:\n          * If random number < 0.2: Generate new solution using GA/Dempster's rule\n          * Else if random number < 0.8: Generate new solution using simulated annealing\n          * Else: Generate random solution\n          * Update solution if new solution is better or random walk accepts it\n          * Periodically evaluate best solution and update global best\n\n  3. Local Descent Phase\n      - At convergence: Use gradient descent with line search\n      - Update equation: new_h = h - \u03b1 * \u2207F(h)\n      - Use numerical gradient estimation (finite difference)\n\n  4. Feasibility projection\n      - After each update: \n          * Scale h to satisfy sum(h)=n/2 \n          * Apply clipping\n      - Use helper function project_feasible()\n\n  5. Termination\n      - Break when time budget exceeded\n      - Return best solution found\n\n  Search choices and useful variations\n  Default parameters:\n      - n_neighbors = {1000: 100, 250: 50, 50: 20}\n      - gradient_steps = 300\n      - line_search_fraction = 0.1\n      - alpha = 0.5 (temperature decay)\n\n  These parameters scale inversely with problem size to maintain efficiency.\n\n  Compute budget and robustness\n  Time allocation: \n      - 40% Global phase\n      - 60% Local phase\n      - Extra 50 seconds reserved\n\n  Use fallback: Sliding window approach if gradient estimation fails\n\n  Validation and return contract\n  - After optimization:\n      * Recompute C5 against reference implementation\n      * Ensure 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)=n/2\n      * Return best_h and corresponding C5\n  - Sanitize output to handle numerical instability\n\n  Global adaptation details:\n    For global search:\n      Genetic operators:\n        - Crossover: n-point crossover\n        - Mutation: Gaussian perturbation with adaptive variance\n      Selection:\n        - Tournaments: size scaled with n\n        - Elitism: keep best 5% across generations\n\n  Local descent details:\n    Gradient estimation with:\n      \u03b5 = 0.001\n      Forward differences for isolated points\n      Central differences for edge cases\n\n  The implementation leverages these search strategies while introducing adaptive parameter tuning specific to this optimization problem.",
